// Stability criteria — dated, pre-registered (METHODOLOGY R6, engine.md).
//
// GENERATION 4, registered 2026-07-31. Generations 1 and 3 remain in git history
// so every certificate scored under them stays reproducible.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE ASSERTS. A region is stable when its metrics stay inside a band
// and their wobble does not grow:
//
//   DRIFTING  LevelRange(max_ratio: 2.2)       did the level stay put?
//   SWINGING  ResidualDamping(max_ratio: 1.5)  did the wobble grow?
//
// LevelRange is the ratio of the 95th to the 5th percentile of the metric over
// the window. Percentiles rather than extremes, so one freak sample cannot
// decide a verdict; and it is deliberately blind to the SHAPE of an excursion,
// because a level that went somewhere and came back is as unstable as one that
// went and stayed.
//
// ResidualDamping removes the fitted log-trend first, so a series is judged on
// how it moves about its own path rather than on where the path went. Spread is
// the interquartile range, not the standard deviation. Its threshold sits ABOVE
// 1.0 on purpose: a real economy in equilibrium fluctuates persistently, and a
// rule demanding decay fails every healthy world.
//
// TWO RETIRED RULES, AND WHY — both were replaced after they were caught scoring
// the wrong thing, and both mistakes are worth remembering:
//
//   Damping(0.72), generation 1. std(2nd half)/std(1st half) of the RAW level.
//   Standard deviation is homogeneous of degree one, so the statistic falls
//   whenever the level falls. It passed a monotone collapse and failed a healthy
//   stationary economy — it rewarded the disease and punished the cure.
//
//   LogDrift(2.0), generation 3. OLS slope of ln(metric) times the window span.
//   That answers "what monotone trend best fits", which is not "did the level
//   stay put". A fit through a V is flat, so it scored a millionfold collapse
//   and recovery as perfectly stable; and it inflated a one-off level shift by
//   exp(delta/2), tripping its own "2x" bar at a true shift of 1.59x. Both were
//   measured on this corpus, not hypothesised.
//
// HOW THE THRESHOLDS WERE CHOSEN — this matters more than their values. Both
// were fixed from the separation between synthetic healthy and synthetic sick
// series in tests/test_09_detectors.rs, BEFORE any scenario was scored with
// them. On the level side, healthy cases (including a business cycle swinging
// +/-30% in logs) reach at most 1.81 and sick ones start at 2.69. On the
// oscillation side, healthy reach 1.04 and sick start at 1.99. A threshold
// picked to make particular scenarios pass is not a threshold (R6), and that
// test is the standing record that these were not.
//
// UNCHANGED ACROSS ALL GENERATIONS: the window (ticks 150..1000), every other
// class, every other threshold, and the fail-closed NaN policy.
//
// CHANGING A THRESHOLD AFTER SEEING RESULTS REQUIRES A NEW DATED GENERATION,
// never a silent edit. The loader reads this filename, so the generation lives
// in the `version` field and the old text in git; `criteria_sha` in the run
// manifest records which bytes actually scored a given run.
(
    version: 4,
    date: "2026-07-31",
    transient: 150,
    analysis_end: 1000,
    staple_good: "flour",
    labour_good: "labour",
    nan_policy: FailClosed,
    classes: [
        // A region with no transaction velocity is not an economy. Checked first
        // and short-circuits: once dead, nothing else about it is meaningful.
        (
            name: "DEAD",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.003, frac: 0.80),
            window: Analysis,
            short_circuit: true,
        ),
        (
            name: "DEAD_EMPLOYMENT",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Employment],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.05, frac: 0.80),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // Short-term oscillation: the rolling coefficient of variation.
        (
            name: "UNSTABLE",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity, Employment, RealWage],
            rule: RollingCv(window: 10, max_cv: 0.12),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // The level must stay put. Split out of v1's SWINGING, which read the
        // raw level and so scored any shrinking series as "settling" (see the
        // header). A metric that moves more than 2x across the window has gone
        // Did the level stay put? The 95th/5th-percentile band across the
        // window, so a metric that went somewhere and came back is as unstable
        // as one that went and stayed, and one freak sample cannot decide it.
        (
            name: "DRIFTING",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity, RealWage],
            rule: LevelRange(max_ratio: 2.2),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // Did the wobble grow? Measured on the detrended residual with an
        // interquartile spread, so this asks only about oscillation and cannot
        // be flipped by a single tick.
        (
            name: "SWINGING",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Velocity, RealWage],
            rule: ResidualDamping(max_ratio: 1.5),
            window: Halves,
        ),
        // Currency pooling in buildings and still trending that way.
        (
            name: "CURRENCY_DRAIN",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [Concentration],
            rule: MeanAndSlope(mean_min: 0.82, slope_min: 0.0),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        (
            name: "POP_DESTITUTION",
            fatal: true,
            metrics: [RealIncome],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.8, frac: 0.50),
            window: Analysis,
        ),
        // Warning only: an idle plant is a symptom, not a failed region.
        (
            name: "DEAD_BUILDING",
            fatal: false,
            metrics: [BldUtil],
            rule: FracBelow(thresh: 0.05, frac: 0.60),
            window: PostTransient,
        ),
    ],
)
